L:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormal/excessive bleeding, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n\nCML Blast Crisis vs Philadelphia Chromosome-Positive Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) with Blastic Crisis:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, abnormal/excessive bleeding, anemia, confusion, easy bruising\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, bleeding easily, blurred vision, enlarged spleen, feeling full after eating small amounts\n\nMyelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Therapy-Related AML vs Philadelphia Chromosome-Positive Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) with Blastic Crisis:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal circulating albumin concentration, abnormal circulating protein concentration, abnormal mean corpuscular volume, abnormality of bone marrow cell morphology\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia: acute disease, acute leukemia of ambiguous lineage, acute myeloid leukemia with mutated npm1\n\nRelated to CML Blast Crisis: chronic myelogenous leukemia, bcr-abl1 positive\n\nRelated to Myelodysplastic Syndrome with Excess Blasts (MDS-EB) / Therapy-Related AML: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, myelodysplastic syndrome, myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts-1\n\nRelated to Philadelphia Chromosome-Positive Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) with Blastic Crisis: blast phase chronic myelogenous leukemia, bcr-abl1 positive, familial chronic myelocytic leuke